-- The CONTROL half of the `…-decl-order` pair, and it is a control in the strict
-- sense: it flips exactly ONE dimension — which of the two same-spelled classes is
-- declared first — and the correct answer is UNCHANGED at 3, for the identical
-- derivation written in the sibling's header.
--
-- MEASURED ON THE B-4b-i BASE BINARY: this ACCEPTED at 3 while the sibling was
-- rejected. That asymmetry is the whole finding. A compiler that selects by spelling
-- passes THIS fixture, which is exactly why it cannot be the only one: an
-- order-decided answer is invisible from whichever ordering happens to be lucky.
--
-- ⚠️ Do NOT "simplify" the two into one fixture, and do not rename the modules to
-- match: the sort order of the module ids IS the variable.
import shared.{Box(..), Opaque(..)}
import amod.{foo}
import zmod.{bar}

main = println (foo (Box Opaque) + bar (Box 1))
